analysis revealed four dimensions: Level of comfort with EHR;
Concern over current ability to monitor medication adherence;
Attitudes about value of DM applications; and Benefits vs cost of
DM for payers. Women scored higher on attitudes about the value
of digital applications (p<0.01). Providers who perceive non-
adherence as costly, and those who believe DM could benefit
providers and patients scored higher on the value of DM (p<.05).
Those whose LSP focuses on improving efficiency and prescribers
with a higher proportion of Medicaid/ uninsured patients dis-
played concern about their ability to monitor adherence
(p<0.05). Willingness to be a Beta Test site for DM applications
was positively correlated with concern about their ability to mon-
itor adherence and attitudes about the value of DM (p <0.01).
Conclusions. Prescriber characteristics including LSP, focus on
healthcare costs, and attitudes toward DM may be related to
adoption of the IEM platform. Those with more Medicaid/ unin-
sured patients were more concerned about ability to monitor
adherence while those focused-on cost and benefit to providers
and patients viewed DM as part of a solution for managing
outcomes and cost. Overall, LSP, patient panel size by payer type,
and focus on healthcare cost containment should be considered
when developing IEM provider training materials.
